<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedict XIV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1675">1675</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prospero Lambertini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benedict XIV, born Prospero Lambertini in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, was pope from 1740 to 1758 and one of the most learned popes of the 18th century. A noted canon lawyer, he reformed the procedure of canonisation, encouraged scholarship and science and kept good relations with European rulers. In 1751 he suppressed the ancient Patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, dividing its territory between the new archdioceses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udine">Udine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv.jpg" alt="Benedict XIV" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Subleyras · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Subleyras_Portrait_of_Benedict_XIV_1746._Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Prospero Lambertini was born into a noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, the third of five children of Marcello Lambertini and Lucrezia Bulgarini. At thirteen he began attending the Collegio Clementino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he studied rhetoric, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, philosophy and theology, and he often read the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, his favourite author. In 1694, at nineteen, he received the degrees of Doctor of Sacred Theology and of both ecclesiastical and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><p>As Promoter of the Faith from 1708 he secured the canonization of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> and wrote a treatise on the process of beatification and canonization of saints. Created a cardinal in 1726, he was named Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1731 and wrote there a three-volume treatise on the diocesan synod, De synodo dioecesana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Clement XII died in February 1740 the conclave lasted six months. Lambertini arrived late and was not among the favourites of any faction; he was put forward as a compromise and is reported to have said, 'If you wish to elect a saint, choose Gotti; a statesman, Aldrovandi; an honest man, me.' He was elected on the 255th ballot on 17 August 1740.</p><p>His pontificate began amid disputes with Catholic rulers over their demand to nominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and he settled the quarrels of the Holy See with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The papal government was heavily in debt, by 56,000,000 scudi, and he cut the army and the pay of its officers and soldiers, though the administration still ran a deficit at his death. He encouraged agriculture and free trade, revived anatomical studies at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and added about 3,300 of his own books to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>.</p><p>On 22 December 1741 the bull Immensa Pastorum Principis excommunicated anyone who enslaved the indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, though it went unenforced. In the bulls Ex quo singulari of 1742 and Omnium sollicitudinum of 1744 he condemned the accommodation of Chinese and Indian customs by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions, and many converts left the Church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Benedict XIV had suffered from kidney problems for years. After a battle with gout he died on 3 May 1758, at the age of 83, lucid to the end, and was buried in Saint Peter's Basilica. He was noted for his modesty and humour and was also an avid gambler who used profane language.</p><p>His intellectualism and advocacy of science education earned him the nickname the Enlightenment Pope. Horace Walpole described him as a pope without nepotism and an author without vanity, a man whom neither intellect nor power could corrupt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Benedict XIV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle Ages the building was not on pilgrims' itineraries, but in 1653 Fioravante Martinelli listed it first among places sacred to the martyrs, and in 1749 Pope <b>Benedict XIV</b> declared it sanctified by their blood, a claim for which there is no historical evidence.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The patriarchate continued as a church office until 1751, when Pope <b>Benedict XIV</b> divided it into the archdioceses of Udine and Gorizia.</blockquote><a href="/state/patriarchate-of-aquileia">Patriarchate of Aquileia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1728" aria-label="Bologna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 590 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Benedict XIV"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5271 860h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5271 860h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1728">What was here in 1728? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
